/* Deep-v4 expansion bundle B: Devi Bhagavata, Naradiya, Markandeya, Agni, Bhavishya. */
(() => {
  const D=window.SCRIPTURE_DETAIL_DATA=window.SCRIPTURE_DETAIL_DATA||{};
  const A=v=>Array.isArray(v)?v.filter(Boolean):(v?[v]:[]);
  const N=s=>String(s||'').toLowerCase().normalize('NFD').replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g,'').replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,' ').trim();
  const E=n=>D['Purāṇa:'+n]||D[n]||null;
  function S(n,t){const e=E(n);if(!e)return null;const xs=A(e.articleSections);let s=xs.find(x=>N(x?.title)===N(t));if(!s){s={title:t,paragraphs:[],bullets:[],subsections:[]};xs.push(s);e.articleSections=xs;}return s;}
  function sub(n,sec,title,ps){const s=S(n,sec);if(s)s.subsections=[...A(s.subsections),{title,paragraphs:ps}];}
  function refs(n,xs){const e=E(n);if(!e)return;e.sources=[...A(e.sources),...xs];const s=S(n,'Further reading');if(s)s.bullets=[...A(s.bullets),...xs.map(x=>typeof x==='string'?x:x.title+(x.detail?' — '+x.detail:''))];}

  /* DEVI BHAGAVATA */
  sub('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa','Structure','Twelve skandhas and the deliberate answer to the Bhagavata model',[
    `The Devi Bhagavata is organized in twelve skandhas, a structure that inevitably invites comparison with the Bhagavata Purana. The resemblance is not merely numerical. Both works frame extensive theology and narrative as a sacred recitation, both claim elevated Puranic status, and both culminate in a form of devotion centered on a supreme personal reality. The difference is that the Devi Bhagavata makes the Goddess, rather than Krishna-Vishnu, the ultimate ground of the cosmos.`,
    `This parallel architecture should not be reduced to a simple act of imitation. It represents a Shakta intervention into an already prestigious Puranic form. The text does not abandon the inherited world of Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva, sages, dynasties and avatara; it reorders that world by making all divine powers dependent on Mahadevi. A familiar Puranic universe is reread from a new theological center.`,
    `The opening skandhas establish narrative frames, cosmology and the problem of divine supremacy. Stories involving Vyasa, Shuka and older Puranic figures give the work continuity with the wider Sanskrit archive. The middle books expand creation, goddesses, sacred geography, dynastic material and ritual, while the seventh contains the celebrated Devi Gita, one of the work’s most important explicit theological discourses.`,
    `The later books move further into ritual, mantra, worship and descriptions of the Goddess’s supreme abode. This progression matters. The Purana does not present metaphysical Shaktism in isolation; it moves from mythic demonstration of the Goddess’s power toward practices by which worshippers can recognize and approach that power.`,
    `Traditional verse totals often give eighteen thousand, mirroring the canonical number assigned to the Bhagavata. As with other Puranas, the number is a marker of scriptural identity rather than a guarantee that every manuscript or printed recension contains an identical count. Chapter numbering is more stable than in some enormous khanda Puranas, but local variation and interpolation still require edition-specific citation.`,
    `The twelve-book structure therefore has a polemical and pedagogical force. It says that a Shakta scripture can encompass everything expected of a Mahapurana—cosmology, genealogy, myth, dharma, pilgrimage, philosophy and liberation—while making the Goddess the reality in whom those domains find unity.`
  ]);
  sub('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa','Contents','Early skandhas: Vyasa, Shuka, cosmic dependence and the problem of who is supreme',[
    `The early narrative frame places the Purana inside the world of Vyasa and his son Shuka, figures already associated with transmission of sacred literature. Their presence lends continuity with epic and Puranic authority while also giving the text room to ask why even famous sages require further teaching. Shakta revelation enters not as rejection of the Sanskrit canon but as its deeper interpretation.`,
    `Creation narratives repeatedly show Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva as powerful yet dependent. In several cycles, the gods confront situations they cannot fully understand until the Goddess or her power is revealed. The theological argument is enacted dramatically: supremacy is demonstrated by the dependence of the familiar male trinity on a more fundamental Shakti.`,
    `The text uses the language of maya with unusual richness. Maya is not simply deceptive illusion opposed to the real Goddess. Divine power is the very capacity through which the one reality manifests multiplicity. The same Shakti can bind beings when misrecognized and liberate when known as the Goddess’s own energy.`,
    `Narratives of cosmic danger and restoration make this metaphysics concrete. Demons, curses and failed divine strategies expose the limits of isolated powers. The Goddess’s intervention restores order not because she is an additional specialist deity but because the powers of the gods themselves are expressions of her.`,
    `The early books also establish a devotional mood in which praise is itself knowledge. Hymns enumerate names and forms, but the multiplicity of epithets is not merely ornamental. Each name marks a way in which the transcendent Goddess becomes intelligible—mother, warrior, knowledge, sleep, prosperity, speech, earth, consciousness or power.`,
    `These sections should be compared with the Devi Mahatmya rather than collapsed into it. Both elevate the Goddess through hymnic and martial myth, yet they are separate texts with different frames and theological development. The Devi Bhagavata absorbs the Shakta breakthrough represented by the Mahatmya and embeds it within a much larger Puranic system.`
  ]);
  sub('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa','Contents','Middle skandhas: goddess myths, sacred geography, kings and the expansion of Shakta narrative',[
    `The middle books show how Shakta theology can inhabit the full range of Puranic narrative. Kings seek offspring or restoration, sages interpret divine events, local goddesses are connected to cosmic Mahadevi, and familiar myths are retold so that the decisive agency belongs to Shakti. The Goddess becomes not one topic among many but the principle by which disparate topics are coordinated.`,
    `Sacred geography is central to that coordination. Places associated with the Goddess, her manifestations or parts of her body form a map of Shakta presence. Lists of pithas and regional shrines allow local cults to be incorporated into a Sanskrit theological network: a goddess worshipped in one region can be understood as a localized manifestation of the one Devi.`,
    `This process does not erase local difference. Individual sites preserve distinct names, myths, ritual specialists and festival calendars. The Purana’s achievement is to make difference legible as manifestation rather than contradiction. A local deity can remain local and simultaneously belong to a transregional Shakta cosmos.`,
    `Royal narratives give the theology political dimensions. Kings depend on divine favor, but the Goddess is not reduced to a guarantor of conquest. Political success can be temporary, and rulers may require renunciation, knowledge or devotion after learning the instability of sovereignty.`,
    `The Purana also contains stories in which women appear as devotees, manifestations or theological teachers. Their roles vary and remain embedded in Brahmanical social assumptions, yet a scripture centered on supreme feminine divinity creates possibilities for religious authority that are structurally different from a text in which female divinity is always subordinate.`,
    `The middle skandhas are therefore best read as the work’s laboratory of integration. Regional goddess worship, dynastic myth, cosmology and household religious concerns are repeatedly translated into the language of one supreme Devi without becoming homogeneous.`
  ]);
  sub('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa','Contents','The Devi Gita: cosmology, devotion, yoga and the Goddess as Brahman',[
    `The Devi Gita, embedded in the seventh skandha, is the Purana’s most concentrated philosophical dialogue and one of the major scriptures of later Shakta Vedanta. Its literary strategy resembles other “Gita” texts: a narrative crisis creates the need for explicit instruction, and the Goddess herself becomes teacher.`,
    `The text identifies the supreme Goddess with Brahman while retaining the possibility of personal devotion. This dual language is fundamental. Ultimate reality is beyond limiting form, yet the same reality assumes forms through compassion so that embodied beings can worship, visualize and love her. Formlessness and divine personality are presented as levels of one Shakti rather than mutually exclusive doctrines.`,
    `Cosmology is explained through Shakti as causal power. The world is real as manifestation of the Goddess even when bondage arises from ignorance of its ground. This lets the text use Vedantic vocabulary without simply reproducing one classical school. Later interpreters can read it through nondual, qualified-nondual or devotional frameworks because its language is intentionally capacious.`,
    `Bhakti receives a high place not as emotional excess but as a disciplined relation that purifies perception. Hearing, chanting, image worship and remembrance create conditions in which the devotee recognizes the divine presence that metaphysics describes.`,
    `Yoga and meditation interiorize the same theology. The body, breath, mantra and subtle centers can become locations of divine awareness. External image and internal visualization are therefore complementary. The Goddess who occupies a temple also occupies consciousness.`,
    `The Devi Gita’s later reception is important because it gave Shakta theologians a compact scriptural source comparable in form to the Bhagavad Gita. Quotation from the Gita can represent the doctrine of the larger Devi Bhagavata, but the full Purana supplies the narrative, ritual and geographic worlds in which that doctrine lives.`
  ]);
  sub('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa','Contents','Later skandhas: mantra, worship, pithas and Manidvipa',[
    `The later books translate supreme-Goddess theology into ritual technologies. Mantra, initiation, image worship, diagrams, vows and festival observance appear as means by which the devotee aligns body, speech and mind with Shakti. These materials show the Purana operating alongside, and sometimes absorbing, concerns familiar from Tantric traditions.`,
    `The presence of Tantric vocabulary should not lead to the claim that every ritual chapter derives from one Tantra or one initiation lineage. Puranic redactors selectively domesticate esoteric forms, presenting them within a public Sanskrit narrative accessible to a wider devotional audience.`,
    `Pitha lists transform geography into a body of the Goddess. The well-known myth of Sati’s dismemberment and the distribution of body parts creates a powerful explanation for why many distant sites can each claim uniquely intense Shakta presence. The one body becomes a network of sacred places.`,
    `Descriptions of Manidvipa and the Goddess’s supreme realm offer a spatial theology of transcendence. Layers of enclosure, divine attendants, jewels, light and the enthroned Devi convert metaphysical supereminence into visualizable sacred architecture. The devotee can contemplate a cosmos whose center is explicitly feminine.`,
    `These descriptions also influence later ritual visualization. Sacred space can be built inwardly through meditation or outwardly through temple and diagram. The Purana’s cosmic palace becomes a template for imagining access to the Goddess beyond ordinary geography.`,
    `The final movement toward the supreme abode provides narrative closure without denying the world. The same Shakti who is the transcendent center has manifested every earlier level of cosmos. Liberation therefore means recognition and relation, not escape from a reality alien to the Goddess.`
  ]);
  sub('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa','Influences and reception','Mahapurana status, Shakta Vedanta and the modern recovery of a major Goddess scripture',[
    `One of the Devi Bhagavata’s most interesting reception histories is its claim to Mahapurana status. Some traditional lists identify the Bhagavata with the Krishna-centered text, while Shakta traditions defend the Devi Bhagavata as the true or equally authoritative Bhagavata Mahapurana. This is not a trivial naming dispute; it reveals competition over canon and the prestige attached to the category “Mahapurana.”`,
    `Later Shakta authors cite the Purana for the supremacy of Devi, pitha geography and theological teachings. The Devi Gita in particular became a portable doctrinal authority that could be read independently while retaining the prestige of the larger work.`,
    `The text also participates in the development of a pan-Indian Shakta sacred geography. Local goddess sites gain textual connections to a common body of Devi, while pilgrimage allows devotees to move among manifestations without treating them as unrelated cults.`,
    `C. Mackenzie Brown’s scholarship was especially important in modern academic study because it treated the Devi Bhagavata as a sophisticated theological work rather than merely a late imitation of better-known Puranas. His analysis of Goddess theology, the relation to Vedanta and the construction of divine femininity remains fundamental.`,
    `Translations and print editions expanded access but also stabilized particular recensions. As with other Puranas, a printed twelve-skandha text can obscure manuscript variation and the history of individual ritual blocks.`,
    `The work’s influence is ultimately larger than any one sectarian doctrine. It demonstrates that the Puranic genre could be fully re-centered around a Goddess while retaining cosmology, genealogy, dharma, pilgrimage and liberation. It is one of the clearest literary documents for the maturation of Sanskrit Shaktism.`
  ]);
  refs('Devī Bhāgavata Purāṇa',[
    {title:'C. Mackenzie Brown — The Triumph of the Goddess',detail:'Foundational study of Devi Bhagavata theology and the development of Shakta scriptural authority.'},
    {title:'C. Mackenzie Brown — The Devi Gita: The Song of the Goddess',detail:'Translation and study of the seventh-skandha theological discourse.'},
    {title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Historical survey of the Devi Bhagavata, its status and relation to the Puranic corpus.'}
  ]);

  /* NARADIYA */
  sub('Nāradīya Purāṇa','Structure','Purvabhaga and Uttarabhaga as two modes of Puranic instruction',[
    `The common two-part recension divides the Naradiya into a Purvabhaga and Uttarabhaga, with the first part itself arranged into four padas. The resulting architecture is not a continuous life of Narada. It is a staged encyclopedia in which the sage’s authority can connect cosmology, devotion, sacred literature, ritual and pilgrimage.`,
    `The Purvabhaga contains the more explicit literary and theological self-consciousness of the work. It teaches Vaishnava devotion, but it also surveys sacred knowledge and includes famous notices about the contents of other Puranas. These catalogues make the text an internal historian of its own genre.`,
    `The Uttarabhaga gives greater space to pilgrimage, vows and sacred calendars. That shift from classification toward practice is significant. After describing a scriptural universe, the text shows how a devotee inhabits it through movement, fasting, gifts, bathing and worship.`,
    `Chapter totals vary enough that edition-specific citation remains necessary. A common printed arrangement counts roughly 125 chapters in the Purvabhaga and about 82 in the Uttarabhaga, but the existence of the Brhannaradiya and other Narada-titled works creates additional bibliographic confusion.`,
    `The title Naradiya can therefore identify more than one textual object in medieval citation. Before using a quotation to date or characterize the Mahapurana, the wording must be checked against the received Naradiya and against the Brhannaradiya tradition.`,
    `The two-part architecture is best understood as a pedagogy of circulation. Narada is the divine traveler and communicator par excellence; the Purana named for him likewise moves among genres and places, turning religious knowledge into something transmitted rather than stored in one institution.`
  ]);
  sub('Nāradīya Purāṇa','Contents','The Purana catalogues: a medieval theory of the Puranic library',[
    `The Naradiya’s summaries of other Puranas are among the most valuable passages in the whole corpus for literary history. They show what one medieval redactor believed texts such as Matsya, Padma, Vishnu or Skanda were supposed to contain. That evidence can be compared directly with surviving recensions.`,
    `Agreement is informative. If the Naradiya describes a major episode still central to a surviving Purana, it can support the antiquity of that association relative to the Naradiya’s own date. Disagreement is equally valuable. A summary may preserve memory of a lost recension, idealize what a Purana ought to contain, or reveal that later manuscripts expanded far beyond the form known to the cataloguer.`,
    `The catalogues therefore cannot be used like modern library metadata. Their purpose is religious as well as descriptive. By enumerating Puranas and their topics, the Naradiya imagines sacred literature as an ordered revelation in which multiple enormous books cooperate.`,
    `The act of summarizing also establishes hierarchy. Certain topics are selected as defining features while thousands of verses disappear from view. What a redactor chooses to mention tells us which parts of a Purana were considered characteristic in that environment.`,
    `These passages became central to modern debates over whether the “eighteen Puranas” known in medieval lists correspond to the texts now bearing those names. Often the answer is only partly. The Naradiya provides a historical snapshot of canon formation, not proof of perfect textual continuity.`,
    `For a scholarly reader, the catalogue section should be used alongside manuscript evidence, citations in dharma literature and internal cross-references. No single Puranic list can reconstruct the corpus by itself, but together such lists map changing ideas of scriptural identity.`
  ]);
  sub('Nāradīya Purāṇa','Contents','Vaishnava devotion: name, hearing, image and the mobility of Narada',[
    `Narada’s literary persona makes him particularly suited to a theology of hearing and recitation. He is a sage who moves between worlds, carrying names, stories and instruction. The Purana turns that mobility into a religious principle: devotion is transmitted through sound and encounter rather than restricted to one place or priestly office.`,
    `Vishnu-Narayana is repeatedly elevated, yet the practice of devotion is broad. Hearing sacred narrative, reciting names, worshipping images, observing vows, visiting tirthas and giving gifts can all become expressions of bhakti. The text therefore represents medieval Vaishnavism as an ecology of practices rather than one emotional state.`,
    `The divine name is especially portable. Unlike a large sacrifice or distant pilgrimage, remembrance can accompany the devotee through ordinary life. This accessibility helps explain the growing Puranic emphasis on recitation in the Kali age.`,
    `Image worship adds a stable local dimension to the same mobile devotion. Consecrated images allow divine presence to be approached regularly, while stories explain their forms and ritual calendars. The Naradiya thus holds together movement and institution: the traveling sage authorizes the established temple.`,
    `Hearing the Purana itself becomes part of the recommended practice. The book’s literary form is therefore reflexive. It teaches that sacred narrative saves while presenting itself as sacred narrative to be heard.`,
    `This is one reason the work can appear encyclopedic without losing devotional coherence. Topics as different as cosmology and pilgrimage are joined by the question of how knowledge of Vishnu becomes lived remembrance.`
  ]);
  sub('Nāradīya Purāṇa','Contents','Sacred time, vows and household religion',[
    `The Uttarabhaga’s calendrical materials make sacred time as important as sacred place. Lunar days, months and festival periods are assigned distinctive practices and fruits. A household calendar becomes a repeated structure of religious opportunity.`,
    `Vratas commonly combine fasting or dietary restriction with worship, story-hearing, gifts and a concluding act. The story attached to a vow explains its power and provides an exemplary performer. Ritual rule and narrative legitimation are thus built together.`,
    `The social accessibility of such observances is notable. While some require resources and Brahman recipients, others emphasize remembrance, simple offerings or disciplined restraint. The Purana can therefore imagine a graded religious economy in which different households participate at different scales.`,
    `Promises of worldly fruit—health, offspring, prosperity, removal of sin—stand beside promises of heaven or liberation. Rather than inconsistency, this reflects a religious system addressing multiple human aims. The text does not assume every devotee begins with pure desire for moksha.`,
    `Sacred months can also connect domestic and temple practice. Lamps, bathing, fasting and recitation may be performed at home or intensified at a shrine. This flexibility helps ritual calendars travel across regions.`,
    `For historians, the prescriptions are normative evidence. They reveal what compilers wished to authorize, not a census of actual practice. Comparison with inscriptions, regional vrata manuals and later nibandhas is needed before converting prescription into social fact.`
  ]);
  sub('Nāradīya Purāṇa','Contents','Pilgrimage as a network of stories rather than a list of destinations',[
    `The Naradiya’s pilgrimage material repeatedly explains why a place matters before telling the reader what to do there. A tirtha is a narrative event fixed in geography: a god appeared, a sage performed austerity, a sin was removed, or a cosmic episode left a trace.`,
    `Pilgrimage therefore becomes a form of reading with the body. Moving among places follows a sequence of stories, while bathing and worship convert remembered narrative into action. A route can function like a chapter order written onto landscape.`,
    `The text’s wide geographic scope creates an imagined sacred India even for readers who never travel everywhere. Hearing names of distant tirthas places them inside a common religious map.`,
    `Regional mahatmyas can be inserted into or extracted from the larger Purana. This modularity is historically important because a local pilgrimage block may have a transmission history different from the rest of the Naradiya.`,
    `Pilgrimage also links renunciant ideals to household practice. A traveler leaves ordinary routines temporarily, adopts restraints and seeks merit, then returns. The journey becomes a controlled form of religious displacement available without permanent renunciation.`,
    `The Naradiya’s sacred geography consequently shows how Puranic religion built networks. Shrines remain local institutions, but Sanskrit narrative allows them to participate in a transregional system of mutually recognizable sacred places.`
  ]);
  sub('Nāradīya Purāṇa','Influences and reception','Narada, canon formation and the afterlife of extracted chapters',[
    `The work’s influence is difficult to measure through full-text copying because later authors often use it selectively. A ritual writer cites one vrata chapter; a historian of Puranas cites a catalogue; a pilgrimage tradition preserves a mahatmya; a devotee encounters a Narada story independently. The text’s reception is modular because the text itself is modular.`,
    `Narada’s broader prestige in bhakti literature strengthened that reception. The Narada Bhakti Sutra is a separate work and cannot be treated as an extract from the Purana, yet both participate in the cultural image of Narada as transmitter of devotion.`,
    `The Purana catalogues became especially influential in modern scholarship. They are repeatedly cited in attempts to date Puranas, reconstruct lost recensions and identify what medieval compilers meant by the canonical eighteen.`,
    `Printed editions had to choose between recensional possibilities and distinguish the Mahapurana from Brhannaradiya. Bibliographic confusion has nevertheless persisted, especially in online repositories where titles are normalized inconsistently.`,
    `The text’s ritual chapters also continued to circulate through regional digests. A later manual can preserve Naradiya authority without reproducing the parent narrative frame, making citation studies essential to reception history.`,
    `The Naradiya is therefore more important than its lower modern profile suggests. It is both a devotional scripture and a witness to the medieval self-understanding of Puranic literature as a canon.`
  ]);
  refs('Nāradīya Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Identity, two-part structure, dating and the Naradiya/Brhannaradiya problem.'},
    {title:'R. C. Hazra — Studies in the Puranic Records on Hindu Rites and Customs',detail:'Ritual chronology and medieval citation evidence.'},
    {title:'Studies of Puranic canon lists and inter-Purana summaries',detail:'Use the Naradiya catalogues comparatively rather than as literal modern tables of contents.'}
  ]);

  /* MARKANDEYA */
  sub('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa','Structure','A frame of questions, exemplary kings and an embedded Goddess scripture',[
    `The Markandeya Purana is structurally distinctive because it lacks the strong sectarian profile of many later Mahapuranas. The sage Markandeya is central to its authority, but much of the work proceeds through nested questions, stories of kings, sages and moral exempla. Its most famous unit, the Devi Mahatmya, occupies only a portion of the whole.`,
    `A common printed recension contains roughly 137 chapters. The opening frame connects Jaimini, a disciple associated with epic tradition, to difficult questions whose answers are transmitted through unusual narrative authorities, including learned birds. This playful framing immediately marks the Purana as a book about the circulation of knowledge rather than a single linear revelation.`,
    `Large narrative blocks include accounts of Harishchandra, Madalasa and other royal or familial figures. These stories explore truth, kingship, renunciation, parenthood and the instability of fortune. Their ethical range helps explain why the Purana cannot be reduced to the Devi Mahatmya even though the embedded Goddess text later became much more famous.`,
    `The Devi Mahatmya commonly occupies chapters 81–93 in the received Markandeya. It has its own internal frame, three major battle cycles and a coherent theology. Its literary independence is shown by the fact that it circulates, is commented on and is recited separately as the Durga Saptashati or Chandi.`,
    `The chapters following the Mahatmya return to the broader Puranic archive. This return matters structurally: the host Purana does not become a Goddess Purana after chapter 93. The Mahatmya is an inserted scripture whose power is intensified by the surrounding nonsectarian frame.`,
    `A deep article must therefore describe two textual objects simultaneously: the Markandeya as a whole and the Mahatmya as a semi-independent embedded work. Confusing them produces both historical and theological errors.`
  ]);
  sub('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa','Contents','Harishchandra and the extremity of truth',[
    `The Harishchandra cycle explores the cost of truthfulness by stripping a king of the conditions that ordinarily make royal virtue possible. Kingdom, wealth, family and status are progressively lost, forcing the ethical commitment to survive without public reward.`,
    `The narrative’s power comes from excess. A simple moral tale could reward truth immediately; instead the king is pushed into humiliating and apparently impossible circumstances. The story asks whether dharma remains binding when the world no longer appears to support it.`,
    `Kingship is thereby separated from possession of a throne. Harishchandra’s royal identity persists as ethical discipline after political sovereignty disappears. This makes the story useful to later traditions interested in satya as a virtue higher than conventional success.`,
    `Family suffering prevents the tale from becoming easy stoicism. The cost of one person’s vow falls on spouse and child, creating a tension between absolute truth and relational duty. The Purana does not hide that cost.`,
    `Divine resolution restores order, but only after the narrative has made human virtue pass through apparent abandonment. The final reward cannot erase the experience of loss that gives the tale its ethical seriousness.`,
    `Later retellings in vernacular literature, drama and popular narrative demonstrate the episode’s portability. Harishchandra became far more than a character in one Purana because the story isolates a universally intelligible conflict between truth and unbearable consequence.`
  ]);
  sub('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa','Contents','Madalasa: motherhood, identity and competing paths of life',[
    `The Madalasa narrative is one of the Purana’s most philosophically striking domestic stories. A queen instructs her children in the unreality or transience of social identity, singing teachings that encourage detachment rather than ordinary royal ambition.`,
    `The setting matters. Renunciant insight is voiced by a mother inside a royal household. The text therefore refuses a simple spatial division in which philosophy belongs only to male forest ascetics while domestic life belongs only to social reproduction.`,
    `Not all sons are treated identically. The narrative eventually has to confront the practical requirement that a dynasty continue. The tension between renunciation and kingship is therefore negotiated rather than solved by abolishing one side.`,
    `Madalasa’s teaching exposes the constructed nature of names and roles. A child is not intrinsically the social identity projected onto him, yet society still requires roles to be inhabited. This paradox resembles broader Upanishadic and yogic critiques of identification while remaining embedded in Puranic family narrative.`,
    `The story later became popular in modern spiritual discourse, often through isolated verses presented as timeless nondual instruction. Its original narrative context is richer: the teaching exists inside an argument about parenthood, succession and the compatibility of knowledge with social duty.`,
    `For gender history, Madalasa is significant but should not be romanticized into proof of general female social autonomy. She is an exceptional literary figure. Precisely as exception, however, she shows that Sanskrit narrative could imagine a woman as authoritative teacher of metaphysical detachment.`
  ]);
  sub('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa','Contents','Devi Mahatmya I: Madhu-Kaitabha and the Goddess as cosmic sleep',[
    `The first major episode of the Devi Mahatmya begins with Vishnu asleep upon the cosmic waters while Madhu and Kaitabha threaten Brahma. The Goddess appears initially as Yoga Nidra, the power of sleep associated with Vishnu. Brahma praises her so that Vishnu can awaken and act.`,
    `This opening already transforms divine hierarchy. If even Vishnu’s waking depends on the Goddess withdrawing, Shakti is not merely the weapon of a male god. She is the condition under which divine agency becomes possible.`,
    `The theology grows through hymn rather than abstract proposition. Brahma names the Goddess as memory, sleep, hunger, power, sacrifice and other pervasive realities. Multiplicity of function establishes omnipresence.`,
    `The demons’ defeat remains part of a Vishnu narrative, but the frame has shifted. The reader has learned to see the Goddess in the very capacities through which the better-known god acts.`,
    `This is why the Mahatmya can be Shakta without erasing Vishnu. It rereads divine power rather than constructing an unrelated universe. Familiar Puranic gods become dependent expressions of Devi.`,
    `The first carita therefore functions as theological overture. Later battles will give the Goddess her own visible warrior forms, but the foundation has already been laid in the invisible power that sustains cosmic consciousness.`
  ]);
  sub('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa','Contents','Devi Mahatmya II–III: Mahishasura, Shumbha-Nishumbha and the multiplication of the one Goddess',[
    `The Mahishasura cycle is the Mahatmya’s most famous martial myth. The gods, defeated and dispossessed, release their energies, which converge into the form of the Goddess. Their weapons become hers, yet the narrative does not leave her as a composite dependent on them. Once manifested, she exceeds the powers that contributed to her appearance.`,
    `Mahisha’s changing forms make the battle a struggle against unstable, excessive power. Buffalo, human and other transformations resist fixed identity. The Goddess’s victory creates the iconographic basis for Mahishasuramardini, one of the most widespread images in South Asian art.`,
    `The Shumbha-Nishumbha cycle develops the relation between unity and plurality even further. Multiple goddesses emerge—Brahmani, Vaishnavi, Maheshvari, Kaumari and others—apparently corresponding to male deities, yet the climax reveals them as emanations who can be reabsorbed into the one Devi.`,
    `The famous challenge that the Goddess fights with the aid of others is answered by theological demonstration: she gathers the forms back into herself and declares her singularity. Narrative sequence becomes an argument that divine plurality does not compromise ultimate unity.`,
    `Kali and other fierce forms intensify the Goddess beyond norms of auspicious femininity. Blood, battle and terrifying laughter become divine. The text therefore expands the range of qualities through which supreme reality can be imagined.`,
    `The concluding hymns promise protection and establish the recitation’s ritual use. The Mahatmya moves from story about a past battle to a scripture capable of intervening in the listener’s present dangers. That transition explains its extraordinary independent ritual life.`
  ]);
  sub('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa','Influences and reception','The host Purana and the independent life of the Durga Saptashati',[
    `The Devi Mahatmya’s reception vastly exceeds that of the rest of the Markandeya Purana. It is recited independently as Chandi or Durga Saptashati, embedded in ritual systems, commented upon through Tantric and Vedantic lenses and performed especially during Navaratri and Durga-related observances.`,
    `This independent life can obscure the host text. A manuscript catalog may list the Mahatmya separately even when its Sanskrit verses correspond to Markandeya chapters 81–93. Scholars must therefore distinguish transmission of the embedded work from transmission of the entire Purana.`,
    `Thomas Coburn’s studies were decisive in showing how the Mahatmya creates a new theological synthesis through older goddess traditions. The text did not invent goddess worship, but it articulated a scriptural claim that many local and Vedic female powers are forms of one supreme Devi.`,
    `Iconography of Mahishasuramardini illustrates reception through image. Sculptures and paintings do not merely illustrate a text; they can precede, parallel or reinterpret textual forms. The mutual reinforcement of image, festival and scripture helped make the buffalo-demon myth culturally ubiquitous.`,
    `The broader Markandeya continued to matter through stories such as Harishchandra and Madalasa, which entered vernacular and ethical traditions independently. Its influence is therefore fragmented across several famous modules.`,
    `The Purana as a whole is a useful warning against measuring importance by sectarian label. A comparatively nonsectarian host became the carrier of one of the foundational scriptures of Sanskrit Shaktism.`
  ]);
  refs('Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Thomas B. Coburn — Devi-Mahatmya: The Crystallization of the Goddess Tradition',detail:'Foundational study of the embedded Goddess scripture, its theology and formation.'},
    {title:'Thomas B. Coburn — Encountering the Goddess',detail:'Translation and interpretation of the Devi Mahatmya and its reception.'},
    {title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Survey of the Markandeya Purana as a whole and its textual position.'}
  ]);

  /* AGNI */
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Structure','An encyclopedia in Puranic form: why its miscellany is the point',[
    `The Agni Purana’s hundreds of relatively short chapters cover a range so wide that modern summaries often degenerate into lists: iconography, temple construction, ritual, geography, kingship, warfare, medicine, grammar, metrics, poetics, omens and more. The better structural question is why such disciplines were gathered under a Puranic title.`,
    `Its answer lies in the genre’s capacity to sacralize useful knowledge. A Purana can frame technical instruction as revelation transmitted through divine and sage authorities. The result is not a modern encyclopedia arranged alphabetically but a compendium in which religious practice, political order and learned sciences belong to one normative cosmos.`,
    `The work is highly layered. Technical chapters can preserve material from specialized sastras and may differ greatly in date and source. One chapter’s relation to an earlier architectural or medical treatise does not date the entire compilation. The Agni is therefore best studied section by section.`,
    `The framing dialogue associated with Agni gives the title unity, but not authorship in the modern sense. The fire god is a transmitter through whom diverse bodies of knowledge are authorized.`,
    `Many chapters are concise enough to look like digests. This suggests a practical function: the Purana could provide portable summaries of disciplines whose full technical literature was much larger. Such digests also facilitate borrowing, because an editor can insert a compact block without reorganizing the whole work.`,
    `The Agni’s structure is therefore archival. Its “randomness” records a historical process in which Puranic redactors continually expanded the boundaries of what sacred knowledge should include.`
  ]);
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Contents','Image making, temple architecture and the material production of divine presence',[
    `The chapters on images and temples are among the Agni Purana’s most historically valuable materials. They discuss proportions, iconographic attributes, forms of deities, installation and the architectural environment in which worship occurs. These prescriptions belong to the same medieval transformation that made permanent temples central institutions of public Hindu religion.`,
    `Iconography turns theology into measured form. A deity’s number of arms, weapons, gestures, posture and attendants identify a particular manifestation. Proportion is therefore not merely aesthetic; it makes an image ritually legible.`,
    `Temple architecture similarly encodes hierarchy. Ground plan, orientation, sanctum, superstructure and surrounding spaces organize movement from ordinary exterior space toward concentrated divine presence. The building becomes a ritual instrument rather than only shelter for an image.`,
    `Consecration is crucial because a carved object is not automatically a worshipped deity. Ritual establishes presence through mantra, purification and installation. The Purana thus joins artisan labor to priestly action.`,
    `Comparison with Agamas, Vastusastras and other Puranas is necessary because the Agni often summarizes technical traditions rather than inventing them. Agreements can reveal shared professional knowledge; differences can show regional or redactional adaptation.`,
    `For art history, these chapters are normative texts, not photographs of actual buildings. Surviving temples frequently deviate from prescriptions. The value of comparison lies precisely in asking when architects followed, adapted or ignored textual ideals.`
  ]);
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Contents','Kingship, fortification, diplomacy and war',[
    `The political chapters place kingship within a religious encyclopedia. A ruler must manage ministers, fortifications, revenue, military organization, diplomacy and punishment, but political technique remains subordinate to dharma and the stability of the realm.`,
    `Material on forts and armies reflects a world of organized warfare in which terrain, walls, weapons and logistics matter. The Purana can therefore preserve technical vocabulary not usually associated with devotional scripture.`,
    `Diplomacy is treated pragmatically. Alliances, enemies, timing and strategic choices recognize that rulers operate in competitive political systems. The presence of such realism beside ritual does not create contradiction; Brahmanical political thought had long accommodated both normative and strategic reasoning.`,
    `Royal ritual and political science also reinforce one another. Consecration gives sacral legitimacy, while successful administration demonstrates the ruler’s capacity to maintain order. A king who performs rites but destroys the realm has failed the larger purpose of kingship.`,
    `The chapters should be compared with Arthashastra, Nitisara and Dharmasastra traditions. Borrowing or shared formulas can identify sources and relative chronology, but direct dependence must be demonstrated rather than assumed from thematic similarity.`,
    `The Agni’s political material is especially valuable because it shows how technical statecraft could be domesticated into a Puranic world. Knowledge of war becomes one branch of sacredly authorized governance.`
  ]);
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Contents','Medicine, body and practical knowledge',[
    `Medical passages summarize diseases, treatments, substances and bodily concerns in a way that brings Ayurveda into the Purana’s encyclopedic orbit. The material is not a substitute for the Caraka or Sushruta traditions, but it demonstrates the circulation of medical knowledge beyond specialist treatises.`,
    `The body appears simultaneously as a ritual, medical and yogic object. Purity rules regulate it, medicine restores it, iconographic analogy can map divine form onto it, and yoga seeks disciplined transformation of its breath and senses.`,
    `Short medical digests are particularly susceptible to textual change because unfamiliar plant names and recipes can be corrupted in copying. Regional identification of substances also varies. A modern reader should therefore avoid treating every recipe as a stable pan-Indian formula.`,
    `Veterinary and practical concerns in encyclopedic Puranas reveal a broad concept of welfare. Animals, agriculture and material resources matter because religious and political order depend on living systems, not only on ceremonial correctness.`,
    `The medical chapters also help date strata when their terminology can be compared with securely dated Ayurvedic literature. Such comparison is more precise than assigning one date to the whole Purana.`,
    `Their inclusion reveals a theory of scripture in which useful knowledge can be religiously valuable. Healing the body is not outside dharma simply because it is technical.`
  ]);
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Contents','Grammar, metrics and poetics: the Purana as a digest of literary culture',[
    `The Agni Purana’s chapters on grammar, meters and poetics are among the clearest evidence that later Puranic compendia could absorb the curriculum of Sanskrit learned culture. A reader encounters not only myths to be narrated but tools for producing and evaluating language.`,
    `Grammatical material condenses an enormous discipline. The purpose is not to replace Panini’s system but to provide accessible rules and categories. This pedagogical compression parallels the Purana’s treatment of medicine and architecture.`,
    `Metrics chapters map the formal possibilities of verse. Because Puranic literature itself is overwhelmingly metrical, the text becomes self-reflexive: it teaches the structures that shape its own medium.`,
    `Poetics and figures of speech reveal engagement with kavya theory. Rasa, guna, dosa and alankara categories connect Puranic narrative to the sophisticated aesthetic discourse of courtly Sanskrit literature.`,
    `These chapters are crucial for chronology because technical vocabulary can sometimes be related to known stages in the history of poetics. Yet redactors may borrow an old definition centuries later, so intertextual evidence must be handled carefully.`,
    `The broader cultural implication is important. The Agni imagines sacred learning and literary learning as continuous. A person educated through this compendium encounters worship, governance and poetry inside one knowledge system.`
  ]);
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Contents','Ritual, mantra and the integration of specialized traditions',[
    `Despite its encyclopedic reputation, the Agni remains deeply ritual. Consecration, worship, vows, rites of protection, mantra and offerings connect technical knowledge to religious action. The compendium does not secularize architecture or medicine; it places them inside a sacred order.`,
    `Mantric and ritual blocks often show contact with Tantric and Agamic traditions. Deity formulas, diagrams and specialized worship can enter Puranic literature in forms accessible beyond closed initiatory texts.`,
    `This process of incorporation should be understood as translation across genres. A technical ritual may be simplified, recontextualized or given a Puranic mythic frame. The Purana is therefore evidence for dissemination, not necessarily the original source of a practice.`,
    `Protective rites demonstrate the practical side of religion. Illness, enemies, bad omens and dangerous transitions receive ritual responses. The sacred is expected to intervene in ordinary vulnerability.`,
    `Domestic and royal rites coexist because the text addresses multiple social scales. A householder and a king may use different resources, but both inhabit the same moral-cosmic order.`,
    `The cumulative effect is a vision of religion as comprehensive competence. Knowing how to build, heal, govern, speak and worship are not isolated domains; they are different forms of ordered action.`
  ]);
  sub('Agni Purāṇa','Influences and reception','The afterlife of an encyclopedic Purana',[
    `The Agni Purana has rarely inspired a single mass devotional tradition comparable to the Bhagavata, but its influence operates through technical extraction. Art historians, ritualists and Sanskrit scholars cite particular chapters because the work preserves compact formulations otherwise scattered across specialized literature.`,
    `Its iconographic chapters became especially important to modern scholarship reconstructing textual norms behind medieval images. Used cautiously, they help identify forms and attributes; used uncritically, they can force diverse regional art into one textual scheme.`,
    `Poetics scholars likewise value the Agni as a witness to the circulation of aesthetic terminology. Its definitions can reveal how elite literary theory entered broader compendia.`,
    `The text’s very heterogeneity made it adaptable in manuscript culture. A compendium can grow by accepting useful blocks without needing to preserve one narrative arc. This likely contributed to variation among witnesses.`,
    `Print culture changed how the work is read by turning hundreds of chapters into a fixed sequential book. Premodern users may more often have consulted or copied selected sections.`,
    `The Agni’s historical significance therefore lies in intellectual integration. It shows what happened when the Puranic idea of sacred knowledge expanded until it could contain much of the practical and scholarly curriculum of medieval Sanskrit culture.`
  ]);
  refs('Agni Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Overview of the Agni Purana’s encyclopedic structure and dating problems.'},
    {title:'R. C. Hazra — Studies in the Puranic Records on Hindu Rites and Customs',detail:'Historical analysis of ritual strata.'},
    {title:'P. K. Agrawala and studies of Puranic iconography',detail:'Comparative use of Agni prescriptions in art-historical research.'}
  ]);

  /* BHAVISHYA */
  sub('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa','Structure','Four parvans and a textual history that reaches unusually late',[
    `The Bhavishya Purana is famous for “prophecies,” but that label obscures its real structure. Received editions commonly organize material into Brahma, Madhyama, Pratisarga and Uttara parvans, each with different thematic and chronological profiles. The work is one of the strongest examples of a Purana that continued to absorb new material into the early modern period.`,
    `The Brahma Parvan contains ritual, calendrical and solar materials that can be much older than the sensational historical passages for which the title is now known. Sacred days, vrata and forms of worship place the text squarely within mainstream Puranic religious instruction.`,
    `The Madhyama Parvan contains additional ritual and religious material whose internal organization differs among editions. Its title does not imply that all of it belongs to one chronological middle layer.`,
    `The Pratisarga Parvan is the most historically controversial because it includes narratives cast as predictions of later peoples, rulers and religious communities. Many passages presuppose the events they “foretell” and are therefore better understood as retrospective prophecy—history written in future tense to claim scriptural authority.`,
    `The Uttara Parvan contains extensive vrata and social-religious instruction. Its ritual world belongs to the same expanding medieval and early modern culture of household observance seen in other large Puranas.`,
    `Because the parvans differ so sharply, any date for “the Bhavishya Purana” without specifying passage is almost meaningless. One block may preserve medieval ritual, another early modern historical memory, and another later regional interpolation under the same title.`
  ]);
  sub('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa','Contents','Brahma Parvan: solar worship, sacred time and a world very different from modern prophecy lists',[
    `The solar emphasis of parts of the Brahma Parvan is a major reminder that the Bhavishya is not fundamentally a catalogue of future events. Surya worship, calendrical observance and ritual instruction occupy substantial space. These materials connect the work to Saura traditions that once possessed a much more visible institutional presence than their modern profile suggests.`,
    `Sacred time is organized through lunar and solar cycles, fasts, gifts and festival days. The “future” implied by the title can therefore be understood partly as calendrical recurrence: scripture tells practitioners what should be done when particular times arrive.`,
    `Ritual chapters often specify material offerings, purity, recipients and promised fruits. Such detail is valuable for comparison with Dharmasastra and vrata digests. It is more securely datable through citation and ritual history than through mythic claims of primordial revelation.`,
    `Solar theology also intersects with royal imagery. The sun’s sovereignty, visibility and life-giving power make Surya an especially suitable deity for kings and public ritual. Historical Saura temples and inscriptions provide external evidence that can be compared with Puranic prescriptions.`,
    `The coexistence of Saura material with later historical prophecy demonstrates the text’s cumulative character. A redactor did not need to erase older ritual chapters when adding new narratives.`,
    `Reading the Brahma Parvan closely therefore corrects a modern distortion: the Bhavishya is first a Puranic religious archive, and only some of its later strata are concerned with retrospective world history.`
  ]);
  sub('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa','Contents','Pratisarga Parvan: retrospective prophecy as a genre of historical interpretation',[
    `Retrospective prophecy is not unique to the Bhavishya. Many ancient and medieval traditions place knowledge of later events in the mouth of an earlier divine authority. The literary form converts political change into evidence that scripture already encompassed history.`,
    `In the Bhavishya this technique is unusually visible because some passages refer to peoples, rulers or religious formations far later than the classical Puranic horizon. Their vocabulary and historical assumptions make it impossible to treat the entire block as a literal ancient prediction.`,
    `Recognizing late composition does not make the passages worthless. On the contrary, they become evidence for how Hindu Sanskrit authors interpreted encounters with new political regimes and religions. A prophecy reveals the perspective of the period that produced it.`,
    `Names are often Sanskritized or distorted, which has encouraged sensational identifications. Responsible interpretation requires philology: manuscript variants, grammar and the wider narrative context must be examined before a modern person or event is matched to a verse.`,
    `The parvan also shows how historical memory can be moralized through yuga theory. Political disruption is interpreted as part of Kali-yuga decline, allowing unfamiliar powers to be fitted into an inherited cosmic chronology.`,
    `Different editions of the Pratisarga do not always contain identical material. This variability is itself evidence that the section remained open to expansion. Claims about what “the Bhavishya Purana predicts” must therefore cite the precise edition and manuscript history.`
  ]);
  sub('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa','Contents','Encounters with Islam, Christianity and later political memory: how to read the controversial passages',[
    `Modern popular literature often extracts verses said to mention Islam, Christianity or specific historical rulers and presents them as miraculous ancient forecasts. Scholarly method begins from the opposite direction: if a passage clearly presupposes later names, institutions or conflicts, that evidence dates the passage’s redactional horizon.`,
    `A Sanskrit author could incorporate information about a new religious community while describing it through older conceptual categories. Such a text is valuable for the history of perception: it shows how unfamiliar traditions were translated into Puranic language.`,
    `The resulting descriptions may be polemical, inaccurate or syncretic. They should not be used as neutral accounts of the communities described. They are Hindu literary representations shaped by the author’s own concerns.`,
    `Some passages may have undergone repeated updating. A short notice can attract elaboration as later copyists or redactors attempt to bring the prophecy closer to their own world. This is one reason manuscript comparison is indispensable.`,
    `Colonial-era readers added another layer by identifying verses with European figures and using them either apologetically or skeptically. Modern debate about prophecy has therefore become part of the Bhavishya’s reception history.`,
    `The academically interesting question is not “did an ancient sage foresee modern history?” but “when, where and why did a Sanskrit Puranic community rewrite later history as foreknown sacred time?” That question produces evidence instead of spectacle.`
  ]);
  sub('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa','Contents','Uttara Parvan and the enormous world of vrata',[
    `The Uttara material returns the reader from geopolitical prophecy to the everyday religious calendar. Vows for prosperity, family welfare, removal of sin and divine favor are narrated and prescribed in forms comparable to other medieval Puranas.`,
    `Vrata literature is often gendered. Some observances are especially associated with women, household welfare or marital aims, while others are open to broader groups. These prescriptions give historians access to ideals of domestic religiosity not visible in elite philosophical texts.`,
    `The ritual is rarely bare procedure. A vrata story explains how the observance worked for a previous performer, creating a model that joins rule and narrative. Failure, suffering and restoration give the rite emotional intelligibility.`,
    `Sacred days and months structure repetitive devotion. Unlike a once-in-a-lifetime pilgrimage, calendrical observance can become a household rhythm transmitted across generations.`,
    `Gifts to Brahmins and other recipients place domestic piety inside wider social exchange. Merit circulates through food, cloth, money, ritual service and storytelling.`,
    `For dating, these chapters should be compared with later vrata digests and citations. The ritual history of one observance may reveal much more than the title “Bhavishya,” which by itself says nothing about chronological depth.`
  ]);
  sub('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa','Influences and reception','From ritual compendium to the internet’s favorite “prophecy Purana”',[
    `Premodern reception of the Bhavishya included ritual use and citation, but modern reception has been dominated by prophecy claims. Printed translations and internet excerpts detach controversial Pratisarga verses from the hundreds of chapters of ritual and sacred-time material that define the wider work.`,
    `This selective reception rewards the most sensational late strata and makes the Purana appear uniquely futuristic. A full article must reverse that distortion by giving structural weight to every parvan.`,
    `The text also became a battlefield in debates over the antiquity and universality of Hindu scripture. Apologetic readings treat apparent references to later religions as proof of omniscience; skeptical readings sometimes respond by dismissing the entire Purana as a modern forgery. Both positions flatten a genuinely layered textual history.`,
    `Philological scholarship offers a more precise account: some layers are older, some much later, and different historical periods continued to write themselves into the Purana. That is not unique fraud but one mode of Puranic textual growth.`,
    `Modern print editions are themselves historical actors because editorial choice determines which controversial chapters reach a mass audience. Comparing nineteenth- and twentieth-century prints with manuscripts can reveal when particular passages became standardized.`,
    `The Bhavishya’s real importance is therefore exceptional evidence for an open canon. Few texts show as clearly how a Sanskrit scripture could continue absorbing new history while preserving old ritual material under one ancient title.`
  ]);
  refs('Bhaviṣya Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Critical discussion of the Bhavishya’s extreme stratification and late materials.'},
    {title:'R. C. Hazra — Studies in the Puranic Records on Hindu Rites and Customs',detail:'Uses ritual and citation evidence to distinguish older and later Bhavishya strata.'},
    {title:'P. V. Kane — History of Dharmasastra',detail:'Comparative evidence for the vrata and calendrical materials transmitted in later Puranic literature.'}
  ]);
})();
